"titre": "Can Humanity Abandon Its Model of Domination Over the Living World?",
"titre2": "Exercise 60",
"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Distinguished ecologists, philosophers, indigenous elders, lawmakers, and fellow citizens, at the root of every environmental crisis confronting our century—whether the warming of the atmosphere, the acidification of the oceans, the exhaustion of fertile soils, or the silent vanishing of wild species—lies a deeper spiritual and philosophical pathology than faulty engineering or inadequate carbon pricing. For centuries, dominant modern civilisations have organised their institutions, technologies, and economies around an adversarial model of domination over the living world: treating nature as a mute adversary to be conquered, dissected, fenced, and forced to yield maximum extraction. Today, as the Earth's life-support systems tremble under the weight of that conquest, a decisive civilisational question confronts us: can humanity genuinely abandon its model of domination over the living world and learn to inhabit the Earth in kinship, reciprocity, and humility?",
"To answer honestly, we must first recognise how deeply the metaphor of domination was woven into the intellectual foundations of modernity. When seventeenth-century thinkers proclaimed that scientific knowledge would render humanity 'masters and possessors of nature', they articulated an emancipatory dream of freeing human beings from famine, plague, and physical hardship. Yet stripped of ethical restraint and ecological humility, that legitimate quest for human security mutated into a totalitarian imperialism toward the non-human world—reducing forests to board-feet of timber, rivers to hydroelectric megawatts, animals to industrial protein units, and complex ecosystems to mere real estate.",
"The fatal flaw of this paradigm of domination is that it rests upon a profound ecological illiteracy: the delusion that a part can conquer and exploit the whole without destroying itself. Human beings do not stand outside the biosphere like an occupying army governing a foreign province; we are woven biologically, chemically, and evolutionarily from the very soil, water, air, and microbial communities we subjugate. Every breath we draw is exhaled by photosynthesising plants and marine phytoplankton; every harvest depends upon unseen pollinators and subterranean fungi. To wage war against the living world is therefore a slow, protracted act of collective suicide.",
"Remarkably, humanity does not need to invent an alternative ethic from nothing, because diverse cultural, philosophical, and indigenous traditions have sustained models of ecological kinship for millennia. Across the Americas, Oceania, Africa, Asia, and the Arctic, indigenous worldviews have long understood rivers, mountains, forests, and animal nations not as inert property, but as living relatives bound to human communities through mutual care, gratitude, and sacred restraint—taking only what is needed, allowing ecosystems time to regenerate, and honouring obligations to seven generations yet unborn.",
"Signs of a profound paradigm shift are already emerging across contemporary science, law, and civic life. Quantum and systems biology increasingly reveal nature not as a blind clockwork machine of selfish competition, but as a symphony of symbiosis, cooperation, and mutual aid. Simultaneously, pioneering courts and parliaments from New Zealand and Ecuador to Colombia, India, and Spain have begun recognising the legal rights of nature—granting rivers, watersheds, lagoons, and forests legal standing to be defended in court against ecological destruction.",
"Abandoning the model of domination in practice requires translating this ethic of kinship into every institution of modern society: replacing extractive industrial agriculture with agro-ecology and rewilding; shifting from linear 'take-make-waste' manufacturing to circular economies modelled on forest nutrient cycles; redesigning cities as living habitats integrated with biodiversity; and educating children not merely to calculate nature's market price, but to know the names, songs, and ecological roles of the creatures sharing their bioregion.",
"Ladies and gentlemen, power measured by the capacity to destroy is the lowest, most primitive form of strength; power measured by the wisdom to nurture, heal, and coexist is the highest mark of human maturity. Humanity stands today at the end of the age of conquest. Let us summon the courage to lay down our weapons against the Earth, step into the great circle of life not as conquerors, but as grateful, responsible kin—and discover that in liberating the living world from our domination, we finally liberate ourselves."
