    "titre2": "Exercise 71",
    "texte": [
      "Sociological and Critical Essay — Modern democratic societies take justifiable pride in having dismantled hereditary aristocracies, legal caste systems, and explicit birthright privileges in favour of formal equality before the law and open meritocratic competition. Yet despite decades of expanded schooling and constitutional equality, empirical sociology consistently documents a stubborn persistence of social stratification across generations: children born into privileged households remain vastly over-represented in elite universities, executive boardrooms, judicial benches, and cultural institutions, while those from working-class backgrounds encounter invisible ceilings at every turn. To explain how inequality reproduces itself without overt legal coercion, the sociologist Pierre Bourdieu formulated one of the most penetrating concepts of modern social theory: 'symbolic domination'—a gentle, largely imperceptible mode of power that operates not through physical force, but through shared cultural classifications, aesthetic tastes, and institutional standards that disguise social privilege as natural merit.",
      "The foundational mechanism of symbolic domination lies in how economic inequality is transmuted into seemingly neutral forms of 'cultural' and 'symbolic capital'. Alongside financial assets, privileged families transmit subtle, embodied dispositions from earliest childhood—an effortless command of standard academic syntax, familiarity with classical art and literature, ease in speaking before authority figures, international travel experiences, and unspoken codes of self-presentation. Because these dispositions are absorbed gradually within the family environment rather than taught explicitly in textbooks, they appear to teachers, employers, and peers alike not as inherited advantages, but as innate 'giftedness', 'charisma', or 'natural intelligence'.",
      "Nowhere is this alchemy of social reproduction more decisive than inside the modern educational system. Far from functioning purely as a neutral social elevator that rewards raw individual talent, schools and competitive examinations routinely evaluate students against implicit cultural benchmarks rooted in the lifestyle of the educated upper-middle class. When an assessment rewards rhetorical polish, abstract detachment, or familiarity with highbrow cultural references, students from privileged homes experience the school as a natural extension of their family world, whereas working-class students must acquire a foreign cultural code while simultaneously being judged on how effortlessly they perform it.",
      "Crucially, symbolic domination achieves its deepest stability only when those disadvantaged by the hierarchy internalise its legitimacy—a process Bourdieu termed 'misrecognition'. Because educational credentials, professional titles, and meritocratic rankings carry the prestigious stamp of objective impartiality, individuals who fail to ascend the social ladder frequently blame their own supposed lack of talent or effort rather than questioning the biased yardsticks by which they were measured. Symbolic violence thus enlists the dominated in their own subordination, transforming structural exclusion into private shame and quiet resignation.",
      "Equally pervasive is how symbolic domination polices everyday boundaries of taste, lifestyle, and moral respectability. Whether in dietary habits, leisure pursuits, musical preferences, or clothing styles, dominant social groups continually redefine what counts as 'refined', 'enlightened', or 'cultivated'—distinguishing themselves from popular tastes dismissed as 'vulgar' or 'unsophisticated'. Even seemingly trivial aesthetic judgments function silently as instruments of social sorting, signalling who belongs effortlessly inside circles of authority and who remains an outsider.",
      "Exposing the invisible mechanisms of symbolic domination does not imply dismissing genuine intellectual effort, artistic excellence, or educational standards as mere illusions. Rather, it demands stripping away the ideological myth that existing hierarchies reflect pure, unmediated nature—and redesigning institutions to become genuinely inclusive. That entails democratising access to rich cultural and linguistic resources from early childhood, broadening educational evaluation beyond narrow sociolects and abstract formalism, and fostering sociological reflexivity among educators, judges, and employers so they recognise inherited privilege rather than mistaking it for innate superiority.",
      "Ultimately, visible chains are relatively easy to recognise and resist, whereas invisible classifications that masquerade as common sense and personal merit are infinitely harder to contest. Bringing symbolic domination into the light of critical awareness is therefore an indispensable act of civic emancipation—reminding democratic societies that true equality of opportunity begins only when we stop confusing inherited cultural advantage with natural human worth."
