"titre": "African Monetary Sovereignty in the Face of the Constraints of Economic Integration",
"titre2": "Exercise 87",
"texte": [
"In-Depth Interview — Interview with Dr. Amadou Diallo, monetary economist and specialist in African regional integration — Journalist: 'Dr. Diallo, few topics ignite fiercer debate among African economists, policymakers, and youth movements than monetary sovereignty—whether concerning the future of the CFA franc zones in West and Central Africa, plans for a single ECOWAS currency, or the broader challenge of escaping dollar and euro dependence. Why is control over money so central to economic sovereignty?'",
"Amadou Diallo: 'Because money is far more than a neutral technical coupon for buying groceries; it is the lifeblood of macroeconomic policy. A sovereign currency allows a state or regional union to conduct counter-cyclical monetary policy during recessions, set interest rates aligned with domestic employment and industrial investment rather than foreign inflation targets, manage exchange rates to protect local manufacturing, and act as a lender of last resort to finance strategic public infrastructure. When a currency is pegged rigidly to an external anchor like the euro—or when trade is overwhelmingly invoiced in US dollars—domestic development priorities are subordinated to external monetary conditions.'",
"Journalist: 'Defenders of fixed currency pegs, such as the CFA franc arrangement, frequently point out that pegging to a strong international currency has delivered low inflation, currency convertibility, and macroeconomic stability compared to neighbours that suffered hyperinflation.'",
"Amadou Diallo: 'That trade-off highlights the classic trilemma of international macroeconomics: a monetary zone cannot simultaneously maintain a fixed exchange rate, free capital mobility, and an independent monetary policy tailored to domestic growth. While a rigid peg to the euro certainly suppressed consumer price inflation, it did so at a steep structural cost: an overvalued exchange rate that penalises local agriculture and manufacturing exports while subsidising imports, chronic credit rationing that starves small and medium-sized African enterprises of affordable long-term loans, and a model geared toward raw commodity extraction rather than structural transformation.'",
"Journalist: 'Yet moving toward autonomous national or regional currencies—such as the proposed West African Eco or a broader African Monetary Union—encounters formidable practical hurdles. Why have regional currency integration projects proved so difficult to realise?'",
"Amadou Diallo: 'Because sharing a single currency across diverse economies without strong fiscal solidarity and economic convergence can actually amplify regional imbalances—a lesson Europe itself learned painfully during the eurozone sovereign debt crisis! West and Central African economies exhibit vastly different fiscal deficits, debt levels, inflation rates, and exposure to commodity shocks—some are oil exporters, others rely on gold, cocoa, or agricultural imports. Launching a common currency prematurely before building harmonized fiscal buffers, regional payment systems, and diversified productive bases risks locking weaker economies into painful austerity.'",
"Journalist: 'How, then, can African economies reclaim meaningful monetary sovereignty while advancing continental economic integration?'",
"Amadou Diallo: 'Through pragmatic, sequenced building blocks rather than abrupt ideological leaps. First, expanding local-currency cross-border settlement platforms like the Pan-African Payment and Settlement System so African traders can transact directly in naira, cedis, shillings, or francs without routing payments through costly US dollar correspondent banks. Second, transitioning rigid external pegs toward flexible, basket-pegged or managed floating regimes backed by pooled continental foreign-exchange reserves and an African Monetary Fund. Third, strengthening regional development banks capable of channelling domestic savings into industrialisation and clean energy.'",
"Journalist: 'In short, monetary sovereignty must walk hand in hand with productive transformation.'",
"Amadou Diallo: 'Precisely: a currency is ultimately only as strong as the real goods, energy, food, and knowledge produced by the economy behind it. True monetary sovereignty means designing financial institutions that serve African production, intra-continental trade, and human dignity rather than external rent extraction.'"
