"texte": [
"Solemn Address — Excellencies, scholars, youth leaders, entrepreneurs, and fellow citizens, we stand at a decisive inflection point in global history. As the post-Cold War unipolar order gives way to a fluid, contested multipolar world—marked by shifting economic gravity, intensifying great-power rivalry, technological disruption, and planetary ecological crisis—Africa is no longer a peripheral spectator on the margins of international affairs. Home to the world's youngest and fastest-growing population, vast reserves of critical transition minerals, immense solar and hydroelectric potential, vital carbon-absorbing rainforests, and dynamic cultural creativity, the African continent stands at the very crossroads of the twenty-first century. Yet a fundamental strategic question confronts African leaders and citizens alike: will Africa enter this reshaping world order merely as a contested prize fought over by external powers, or as a unified, sovereign pole authoring its own destiny?",
"To seize this historic moment, we must first look candidly at the perils of the 'new scramble for Africa'. Today, traditional Western powers, rising Asian giants, Gulf monarchies, and middle powers compete intensely across the continent for diplomatic alliances, military bases, port concessions, digital infrastructure contracts, and strategic minerals. While diversified partnerships offer welcome alternatives to former colonial monopolies, bilateral fragmentation remains Africa's greatest vulnerability: when fifty-four states negotiate separately with superpowers, external actors easily extract asymmetric concessions while exporting geopolitical rivalries onto African soil.",
"Conversely, Africa possesses extraordinary structural assets that, if marshalled through collective strategy, confer immense global leverage. By mid-century, one in four human beings on Earth—and nearly forty percent of the world's youth—will be African. In an ageing global economy facing acute demographic contraction across Europe and East Asia, Africa's youthful energy, urbanising markets, and creative talent represent the single greatest reservoir of future human dynamism. Yet demographic youthfulness is not an automatic 'dividend'; without massive, transformative investment in quality education, technical research, healthcare, and decent job creation, a demographic boom risks turning into a crisis of frustration and instability.",
"Equally decisive is Africa's pivotal role in the planetary ecological and energy transition. Hosting the Congo Basin—the Earth's largest remaining tropical carbon sink—alongside abundant lithium, cobalt, copper, and unmatched solar irradiance, Africa holds keys indispensable to global climate survival. However, the continent must firmly refuse to serve merely as a low-value quarry supplying raw green minerals for Northern decarbonisation while hundreds of millions of Africans still lack basic electricity. True climate partnership requires industrialising Africa through green energy, local mineral refining, regenerative agriculture, and fair global climate finance.",
