"titre": "Does Humanity's Moral Progress Necessarily Follow Its Scientific Progress?",
"titre2": "Exercise 92",
"texte": [
"Historical and Ethical Chronicle — During the height of the European Enlightenment and the nineteenth-century industrial boom, visionary philosophers and scientists shared an intoxicating conviction: that the expansion of empirical science, rational education, and technological mastery would inevitably elevate human morality alongside material comfort. Ignorance, superstition, and cruelty were viewed as twin relics of a dark past destined to evaporate under the steady light of scientific reason. Yet the blood-soaked trajectory of the twentieth and early twenty-first centuries delivered a devastating refutation of that linear optimism: the very century that unlocked quantum mechanics, antibiotics, spaceflight, and digital computing also witnessed industrialised genocide, total war, nuclear incineration, colonial massacres, and accelerating planetary ecocide. History confronts us with an unavoidable philosophical truth: moral progress does not follow automatically from scientific progress.",
"The fundamental reason for this divergence lies in the distinct nature of scientific knowledge versus ethical cultivation. Science investigates how the physical and biological world operates—expanding human power and instrumental efficiency—yet empirical equations remain inherently silent on what ends ought to be pursued or what duties we owe to vulnerable beings. A breakthrough in chemistry can synthesise life-saving fertilisers or lethal poison gas; nuclear physics can illuminate cities or vaporise them; genomics can cure hereditary disease or enable discriminatory eugenics. Science multiplies capability without automatically supplying conscience.",
"Indeed, when divorced from ethical restraint and democratic accountability, scientific and technological sophistication can actually amplify human cruelty by stripping away visceral moral inhibition. Psychologists and historians of modern atrocities—from Zygmunt Bauman in Modernity and the Holocaust to scholars of remote drone warfare—have demonstrated how bureaucratic division of labour, technical abstraction, and physical distance enable ordinary individuals to participate in mass destruction without ever confronting the human face of their victims. Pressing a button on a screen or optimising a logistics spreadsheet anesthetises the empathetic revulsion that face-to-face harm naturally triggers.",
"Furthermore, the institutional structures funding and directing modern science frequently subordinate inquiry to commercial profit or military supremacy rather than universal human flourishing. Billions flow effortlessly into weapons engineering, addictive attention algorithms, and luxury consumer enhancements, while neglected tropical diseases, affordable clean water, and ecological restoration remain chronically underfunded. Left purely to market or strategic incentives, scientific advancement mirrors existing hierarchies of power rather than correcting moral injustice.",
"Nevertheless, rejecting naive technological determinism does not mean denying that genuine moral progress is possible—or that science, when guided by ethical vision, can powerfully support moral expansion. Over recent centuries, humanity has achieved hard-won ethical breakthroughs: the legal abolition of chattel slavery, the codification of universal human rights, the expansion of women's suffrage, growing recognition of decolonisation and indigenous dignity, and emerging legal protections for non-human animals and ecosystems. In many instances, scientific discoveries—demonstrating the biological unity of all human races, uncovering animal consciousness, or revealing planetary ecological interdependence—provided vital empirical foundations for expanding our circle of moral concern.",
"Aligning scientific progress with genuine moral advancement therefore requires deliberate cultural, educational, and institutional work. Ethics cannot be treated as an ornamental afterthought tacked onto the end of technical training; scientists and engineers must be educated as reflective moral citizens versed in history, philosophy, and social justice, while democratic publics and international treaties establish binding ethical boundaries governing research and deployment.",
"Ultimately, science gives humanity wings, yet only moral wisdom can teach us where to fly. Whether our growing mastery over matter, life, and intelligence becomes a blessing for all living beings or an instrument of refined barbarism will never be decided in a laboratory alone—it will be decided in the human heart, in our laws, and in our daily commitment to justice and compassion."
