"titre": "Can Material Abundance Coexist with Existential Poverty?",
"titre2": "Exercise 93",
"texte": [
"Existential and Sociological Essay — Across the affluent societies of the early twenty-first century, citizens enjoy levels of material comfort, technological convenience, consumer variety, and life expectancy that would have astonished monarchs of earlier centuries. Climate-controlled homes, global air travel, instant access to every film or melody ever recorded, and year-round abundance in supermarkets have transformed daily existence. Yet alongside this unprecedented material plenty, sociological and psychological indicators reveal a haunting paradox: rising rates of chronic anxiety, depressive emptiness, substance dependency, epidemic loneliness, and a pervasive sense of purposelessness. Confronted with societies overflowing with commodities yet starved of meaning, philosophy and social thought must pose an urgent question: why does material abundance so frequently coexist with profound existential poverty?",
"The first source of this paradox lies in a fundamental confusion between 'having' and 'being'—a distinction incisively explored by thinkers from Erich Fromm and Gabriel Marcel to Viktor Frankl. Modern consumer capitalism systematically trains individuals from childhood to translate every spiritual, emotional, or relational longing into a commercial purchase: seeking belonging through luxury brands, self-worth through status possessions, and inner peace through endless entertainment. Yet material commodities can satisfy only physical and instrumental needs; when asked to fill an existential void of meaning, love, or moral purpose, consumption delivers a fleeting dopamine spike followed by deeper emptiness.",
"Reinforcing this existential impoverishment is the relentless commodification of human time and attention. Within hyper-productive economies obsessed with efficiency, speed, and perpetual busyness, unhurried contemplation, silent wonder, deep friendship, and civic engagement are treated as unproductive luxuries. Exhausted by long commutes, precarious schedules, and continuous digital notifications, individuals possess more material gadgets than ever before while suffering acute 'time-poverty'—deprived of the unscripted, uncommodified hours in which human beings discover who they are and what truly matters.",
"Equally corrosive is the erosion of transcendent horizons and shared ethical narratives in hyper-individualised consumer societies. As Viktor Frankl observed in Man's Search for Meaning, human beings thrive not when freed from all tension or responsibility, but when summoned by a purpose larger than the isolated ego—whether service to others, artistic creation, ecological stewardship, scientific truth, or solidarity with future generations. When dominant culture reduces the supreme goal of life to private utility maximisation and personal comfort, the individual is left trapped inside a cramped, self-referential universe that quickly grows suffocating.",
"Simultaneously, material abundance in affluent societies frequently rests upon invisible moral contradictions that quietly unsettle the conscience. Knowing—even dimly—that cheap fast fashion, disposable electronics, and hyper-consumption rely on exploited labour abroad and accelerating ecological devastation at home breeds a subtle bad faith, alienating individuals from their own ethical integrity.",
"Healing existential poverty amid material plenty does not require glorifying physical destitution or romanticising pre-industrial hardship; dignified material security—nutritious food, safe shelter, healthcare, and education—is an indispensable foundation for human flourishing. Rather, it requires reorienting society from quantitative accumulation toward qualitative richness: shortening working hours so productivity gains liberate time for family, art, and community; designing cities around beauty, nature, and public gathering rather than shopping malls; cultivating contemplative and philosophical education; and rediscovering the joy of contribution, gratitude, and sufficiency.",
"Ultimately, human beings do not live by bread—or bandwidth—alone. A civilisation that fills every warehouse with commodities while leaving human souls isolated, hurried, and purposeless is affluent in appearance yet impoverished in essence. True prosperity begins when we learn that once basic physical needs are met, the richest treasures of human life—meaning, presence, beauty, solidarity, and love—cannot be bought, only shared."
