    "titre": "Is the Pursuit of Absolute Security a Threat to Freedom?",
    "titre2": "Exercise 94",
    "texte": [
      "Constitutional and Political Essay — Across modern political philosophy—from Thomas Hobbes's Leviathan to contemporary debates over digital surveillance, predictive policing, and emergency executive decrees—few tensions have proven as perennial as the equilibrium between security and liberty. Every legitimate political community owes its citizens basic protection against arbitrary violence, foreign aggression, and social chaos; without minimal physical and legal security, freedom remains an empty abstraction enjoyed only by the strong. Yet when modern states and frightened publics elevate security from an essential baseline into an absolute, totalising imperative—demanding the elimination of every conceivable risk prior to harm—the pursuit of total security quietly devours the very constitutional liberties, privacy, and civic trust it claims to defend.",
      "The first mechanism through which absolutist security erodes freedom lies in the logic of pre-emptive suspicion. Traditional criminal justice under the rule of law operates retrospectively and individually: the state may intrude upon a citizen's privacy or liberty only upon specific, probable cause of unlawful conduct, subject to independent judicial review. By contrast, twenty-first-century 'preventive security'—supercharged by mass biometric scanning, algorithmic risk profiling, automated communication interception, and administrative watchlists—treats the entire population as latent suspects. In seeking to detect threats before they crystallise, pre-emptive surveillance inverts the presumption of innocence into a presumption of potential danger.",
      "Equally corrosive is the normalisation of permanent states of emergency. As the jurist Carl Schmitt notoriously observed and democratic theorists from Giorgio Agamben to Bruce Ackerman have warned, crises—whether terrorist attacks, financial panics, or public-health emergencies—create powerful executive incentives to bypass parliamentary deliberation, curtail freedom of assembly, and suspend due process. While temporary, strictly proportionate emergency powers may occasionally prove necessary, history demonstrates that extraordinary powers rarely retreat once the immediate storm passes; instead, exceptional measures calcify into routine administrative statutes, steadily ratcheting down the baseline of constitutional liberty.",
      "Simultaneously, the pursuit of absolute security reshapes public psychology and civic culture by fostering a climate of chronic fear and mutual distrust. When political leaders and sensationalist media continuously amplify existential threats to justify expanded policing or border militarisation, citizens internalise a siege mentality—viewing strangers, political dissidents, and minority communities not as fellow rights-bearing equals, but as potential vectors of hazard. A society obsessed with zero risk loses the civic courage required for open debate, peaceful protest, and democratic experimentation.",
      "The bitter irony of pursuing absolute security is that total safety is an ontological impossibility in human affairs—and attempting to engineer it through unchecked state power creates a far greater danger than the risks it seeks to avert. History offers an unmistakable verdict: concentrated, unaccountable coercive apparatus built to protect citizens against external threats or crime has repeatedly become the primary instrument of domestic tyranny, political persecution, and abuse of power.",
      "Reconciling security with freedom in a complex century does not mean indulging naive pacifism or leaving societies defenceless against genuine threats. Rather, it requires anchoring security policy inside strict constitutional boundaries: insisting upon narrow, judicial-warrant-backed surveillance rather than indiscriminate dragnet collection; attaching automatic sunset clauses and parliamentary oversight to any emergency powers; protecting independent courts, whistleblowers, and investigative journalism; and recognising that social security—decent housing, healthcare, education, and economic dignity—prevents far more instability than militarised policing ever could.",
      "Ultimately, as Benjamin Franklin famously warned, those who would give up essential liberty to purchase a little temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. Freedom inherently entails living with a measure of irreducible uncertainty and vulnerability. A mature democracy refuses the seductive mirage of the risk-free police state—choosing instead the courageous balance of a free society that protects its citizens without turning them into prisoners of their own protection."
