"titre": "The Collapse of Certainties as a Condition for the Emergence of New Paradigms",
"titre2": "Exercise 99",
"texte": [
"Epistemological and Historical Chronicle — Human beings naturally crave conceptual stability: coherent worldviews, trusted economic dogmas, settled scientific models, and predictable institutional maps that render an intricate universe intelligible and reassuring. Yet across the long history of science, philosophy, and civilisation, periods of profound intellectual renewal have almost never arisen from the smooth, incremental accumulation of familiar truths. Rather, transformative breakthroughs occur precisely when inherited frameworks encounter stubborn anomalies they can no longer explain—triggering a disorienting 'collapse of certainties'. As the historian of science Thomas Kuhn famously demonstrated in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, the unravelling of an entrenched worldview, however unsettling in the short term, is the indispensable precondition for the birth of a deeper, more adequate paradigm.",
"In Kuhn's classic account, 'normal science' operates comfortably within an established paradigm—Ptolemaic astronomy, Newtonian mechanics, or classical equilibrium economics—solving routine puzzles while ignoring or patching over minor discrepancies. Over time, however, empirical anomalies accumulate until ad hoc adjustments collapse under their own weight: planetary orbits refuse to fit epicycles, subatomic particles defy classical determinism, or ecological breakdown and financial crashes refute models assuming infinite growth and self-correcting markets. The resulting paradigm crisis feels initially like chaos, nihilism, or loss of orientation, yet it performs an essential creative destruction—freeing human imagination from dogmatic blinders.",
"This dynamic extends far beyond physics and chemistry into political economy, ethics, and civilisational self-understanding. Whenever an entire historical order exhausts its explanatory and moral credibility—whether the divine right of feudal monarchs during the Enlightenment, colonial racial hierarchies during twentieth-century decolonisation, or fossil-fuelled growth dogmas in the Anthropocene—the collapse of old certainties opens an ideological interregnum. What Antonio Gramsci observed of political transitions holds equally true of intellectual revolutions: the old world is dying, the new world struggles to be born, and in the twilight between them, both morbid symptoms and extraordinary creative possibilities appear.",
"The acute danger during an era of collapsing certainties is the psychological temptation to flee anxiety either into reactionary fundamentalism or into cynical despair. Frightened by the loss of familiar landmarks, some cling ferociously to discredited dogmas—denying climate science, romanticising authoritarian nostalgia, or enforcing ideological orthodoxy—while others conclude nihilistically that because old truths failed, no truth or progress is possible at all. Both reactions mistake the death of a limited historical model for the end of human reason itself.",
"The subtle irony of intellectual history is that our most liberating discoveries often begin with the humbling admission 'our map no longer matches the territory'. When Albert Einstein questioned absolute Newtonian space and time, when ecological economists exposed the blind spots of gross domestic product, or when post-colonial thinkers dismantled Eurocentric universalism, their courage to inhabit uncertainty unlocked richer, more accurate ways of perceiving reality.",
"Navigating the collapse of certainties constructively therefore requires cultivating 'paradigmatic courage'—the intellectual and civic poise to treat crisis not as an apocalypse, but as an invitation to structural reinvention. That demands protecting open, non-dogmatic inquiry in universities and public discourse; encouraging cross-disciplinary dialogue where fresh conceptual metaphors emerge; listening attentively to marginalised voices long excluded by dominant paradigms; and testing emerging frameworks rigorously against empirical evidence and human dignity.",
"Ultimately, certainties are like scaffolding erected around a building under construction: indispensable while we climb, yet destined to be dismantled so the broader structure can stand revealed. Far from signalling civilisational exhaustion, the collapse of outdated certainties marks the threshold of renewal—offering humanity the rare, precious freedom to reimagine its knowledge, its institutions, and its shared future anew."
